# Vercel serves the deployment root as static files. This repo stays private until
# submission on 13 Sep, so ship only what the page actually fetches: app/, the
# replay CSV it reads, and the README. Everything else — the contracts, the tests,
# the scripts, the keeper — is the edge, and it goes public on our schedule, not
# because a static host happened to serve it.
#
# TWO TRAPS, both already hit once:
#
# 1. Every pattern must be ROOT-ANCHORED with a leading slash. Without it these are
#    gitignore patterns matching at any depth, and a bare "src/" silently deletes
#    app/src/ as well — which is what happened on the first deploy: the page shipped,
#    every module 404'd, and it hung on "connecting to HyperEVM…".
#
# 2. This file does NOT inherit .gitignore, and `vercel --prod` uploads the working
#    directory rather than the git tree. So a secret git never sees still reaches a
#    public host unless it is named here.
# The CLI excludes it by default, but this file is the one that has to be right: the repo is
# private until submission and `vercel --prod` uploads the working directory, not the git tree.
/.git
/.env*
/.vercel
/deployments/31337.json
/src/
/test/
/script/
/keeper/
/subgraph/
/lib/
/out/
/cache/
/broadcast/
/node_modules/
/tape/
/.github/
/foundry.toml
/remappings.txt
/package.json
/yarn.lock

# the Substreams package: a Rust crate and a 230KB wasm module. Vercel serves the page, and the
# page reads results/markouts.json — the keeper's output, not its build tree.
/substreams/
